    "total_in_level": 10,
    "title": "Dequeísmo, Queísmo and Verb Government",
    "subtitle": "which verbs take de, en, a or con before que, conjunctions with de, and personal a",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · C2 · Grammar · Verb government",
    "next": "Next: C2 · PDF 5 — Rhetoric and figures of speech"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "tell at once whether a verb needs de, en, a or con before que",
        "avoid dequeísmo (extra de) and queísmo (missing de)",
        "use personal a correctly, including the borderline cases",
        "use the question test to check any doubtful sentence"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. The problem"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "<b>Dequeísmo</b> = adding <i>de</i> where it is not allowed (*Pienso de que...). <b>Queísmo</b> = dropping <i>de</i> where it is required (*Me alegro que...). Both are common in speech; at C2 you must get them right."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "The question test",
      "bullets": [
        "Turn the sentence into a question about the clause.",
        "If the question needs the preposition (<i>¿De qué te das cuenta?</i>), keep de: <i>Me doy cuenta de que...</i>",
        "If the question works without it (<i>¿Qué piensas?</i>), drop de: <i>Pienso que...</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
